From: Antigravity <local@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] linux-user: default guest_base to 0x800000000000 for x86_64

On 64-bit hosts with virtual address spaces larger than 47 bits (such as
ARM64 with CONFIG_ARM64_VA_BITS=48), guest x86_64 allocations via host mmap
frequently return addresses >= 2^47 (bit 47 = 1).

x86_64 user-space allocators and runtimes (including jemalloc in rustc/uv
and mimalloc in Nix) assume mmap() without a high hint returns addresses
below 2^47, sign-extending or tagging pointers based on bit 47. When bit 47
is set, sign-extension creates non-canonical addresses, resulting in SIGSEGV.

Defaulting guest_base to 0x800000000000 when unspecified maps the guest
address space cleanly into the host's upper 48-bit space and restricts guest
VMAs allocated by mmap_find_vma to < 2^47.
---
 linux-user/main.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/linux-user/main.c b/linux-user/main.c
index 1234567..89abcdef 100644
--- a/linux-user/main.c
+++ b/linux-user/main.c
@@ -957,6 +957,13 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp)
 
     optind = parse_args(argc, argv);
 
+#if defined(TARGET_X86_64)
+    if (!have_guest_base && !reserved_va) {
+        guest_base = 0x800000000000ULL;
+        have_guest_base = true;
+    }
+#endif
+
     target_environ = envlist_to_environ(envlist, NULL);
     envlist_free(envlist);
-- 
2.43.0
